<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Mauritania</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Nouakchott</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Saharan country in West Africa, independent from France in 1960, run by coups and military rulers before a peaceful change of president in 2019.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-mauritania">French Mauritania</a> was part of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, and became an independent nation in 1960. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nouakchott">Nouakchott</a>, then little more than a fortified village, as the capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moktar-ould-daddah">Moktar Ould Daddah</a>, originally installed by the French, became the first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1964 Ould Daddah made Mauritania a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> under a new constitution. He was ousted in a bloodless coup on 10 July 1978 after the war to annex part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>, and military committees ruled until Colonel Maaouya Ould Sid'Ahmed Taya took power in December 1984. Opposition parties were legalised and a new constitution was approved in 1991. Taya was overthrown in a coup on 3 August 2005. Mauritania's first multi-candidate presidential election was held on 11 March 2007, and another coup followed in 2008. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> was abolished officially in 1905, 1981 and 2007, but it still exists.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>General Mohamed Ould Abdel Aziz won the presidency in 2009, after leading the 2008 coup. Mohamed Ould Ghazouani was sworn in in August 2019 in what was considered the first peaceful transition of power since independence, and was re-elected in June 2024. Abdel Aziz was arrested in June 2021 and sentenced in December 2023 to five years in prison for corruption.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mauritania.jpg" alt="Mauritania" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mauritania.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nouakchott</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Moktar Ould Daddah, Maaouya Ould Sid'Ahmed Taya, Sidi Ould Cheikh Abdallahi, Mohamed Ould Abdel Aziz, Mohamed Ould Ghazouani</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam (almost all Sunni)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, with Pulaar, Soninke and Wolof as national languages</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mauritania">Mauritania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Islamic Republic of Mauritania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=mauritania" aria-label="Mauritania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4247 1507 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mauritania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4567 1763L4567 1763"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4567 1763h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4567 1763h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4567 1763h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nouakchott. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=mauritania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
